# One body, explained a step at a time

A literate file's program is its lines indented four columns. Prose between two of them ends
nothing, so this function's body runs on across the paragraphs below.

    // expect: exit 3

    main() -> int
        var n = 0
        var total = 0

The loop's body is two columns deeper than the function's, and the prose that interrupts it is at
the margin. Neither moves the program's own indentation.

        while n < 3 do
            n += 1

            total += n

A blank line inside a block, and one with only spaces, is still a blank line:

        println(s"total: $total")

```
main() -> int = 99   // an illustration: never compiled
```

~~~~
    a fenced block indented four columns is still an illustration
~~~~

- a list item may hold an example of its own:

      main() = "not the program either"

The last line of the body comes after all of that.

        n
